    "turn_idx": 26,
    "turn_text": "the people say, because it's a different, you know, sometimes government appoints, sometimes it's actually much harder to convince 100 informed people than 100,000 not very well informed people to actually to get their vote. But I'll be honest with you, I've been in business. I was a Fortune 500 auditor for Big Four, and I had lots of different clients all over the country, all over the world, and a variety of industries. And I could tell that health care is a huge problem. You know, I could see it as being a finance professional. It's really big part of federal state family employers budget and the quality is not there, but price is out of control and spend is out of control. So when I became a legislator, I actually I did a program at Harvard Business School. It was one of my colleagues and friends that does some actually kind of very famous in the county finance world. He's the one who co-funded, balanced school card, activity based costing. So I kind of was going to work on some different stuff. But then I looked and he was doing some on health care costs with Dr. Kaplan and Dr. Porter. So I thought, well, I'm going to visit them and do some executive education with them just to see what they do and then kind of how they look in price and value competition. So I did that. And then I started discussing with my legislators. I said, we have to really deal with that. We have to do it. So I kind of legislated some some studies. It's very difficult to deal with complex issues. I was like, oh, yes, we need no one want to do it. Then as that working with employers forums, they did some study on price transparency. And Indiana happened to be the highest from 25 states that they studied on health care prices. So I shared that stuff with my colleagues, the governor said, you see, we have to do something about it. I told you,",
