  {
    "episode_id": 50750,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50750.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 40,
    "turn_text": "But it's interesting because one side's never fits, one side's never fits all. And I think I honestly believe a lot of federal people start realizing that too, so they do a lot of drama to justify the existence, but honestly a lot of them are looking, including administration, to give more flexibility to the states, and they understand that all of them innovation, all of the solutions are going to be at the states, and I hope, as the states, we're going to be more proactive and push on that and not subrogate our rights to the federal government because there are a few functions that they should be doing. And I believe it's going to be pushed down to the states. You can see even with an issues, with all the gopples, monopolies, anti-trust, they take initiatives because people see us, people get into our face, we have real problems, we don't sit in this scene, the fancy, fancy steak dinners, we actually hear on the ground so they can get to us and we start looking at issues. I think it's become more urgent. I'm glad it is, and I do believe the best thing they can do. And I think a lot of them will start doing it, hopefully, at some point when they stop doing drama, actually to de-regulate. And so this regulation doesn't tell state what to do because each state has a different unique needs, too. And we cannot say that my state is the same as the state of California. I'm glad it's not, or Illinois.",
