    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/0/50750.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 47,
    "turn_text": "Sure, sure. I totally agree with that. And that's one of the biggest issues within direct care, obviously, where Freedom Health Works comes in, and we're big proponents of that, and stripping out any type of middlemen with our insurance or government. But within direct primary care, there's this thinking that's a federal level. With a stroke of a pen, the feds can fix everything and open up this beautiful market and allow people to purchase medicine or purchase healthcare services for transparent pricing and all this cool stuff. But everything's focused on making HSAs compliant for purchasing direct care, rather than opening it up and saying, why are we even regulating how people spend their dollars? And for something like a direct care movement, you know, what we need in there is actual doctors. We need the people who are practicing medicine to open it up and say, Hey guys, there's a blue ocean over here. There's a lifestyle. This is a better living. This is better care for your patients. And. Indiana has been one who recognized that from very, very early. And I know that you're working on certain proposals like that. And we've had discussions on physician non-competes. We've had discussions on making pricing more transparent. And you've been very, very receptive of that, which I appreciate. But you've also said, this is part of a more comprehensive approach. And I wanted to get your thoughts on what does that comprehensive approach look like that you're working on right now?",
