    "turn_idx": 63,
    "turn_text": "I love it. It's taking the power away from, like you said before, the oligarchy, and the insurance companies and the government who represent the third-party payers in putting all that power back into consumers. As consumers, we have to educate ourselves and say, okay, we're actually going to shop around for health care. And we've had guests on the show who say, I'd even know that was a thing. It even crossed my mind. I thought that these health systems had my best interest in mind when in reality, you look at the bottom line, you look at the prices and the costs and what they're charging you and saying, holy cow. This was even, I didn't even think to ask that. And so, you know, they're, again, back to the education gap that with great power comes great responsibility, you know, to quote Spider-Man or whatever the heck that movie was, but it's like you have to empower yourself as a consumer. If these things, if your work is all geared towards giving power back to consumers and then helping physicians along the way too, because too often they're treated as culprits when really they're innocent, biased energy over there saying, I wish I could help you, but I can't.",
